# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# issues/ is WHITELIST-governed, not blacklist.
#
# Blacklisting failed twice in one day: `git add issues/8605 issues/8606
# issues/8607` for three edits committed 634 files, including an 8.9 GB
# _work.zip that GitHub silently refused for every push afterwards.  A
# blacklist only stops the intermediate you already thought of; the build
# writes new ones (_cache/, png/, png2/, Archive.zip, *.tsv) whenever a stage
# is added.  So: ignore everything one level inside an issue dir, then name
# what a PUBLISHED issue actually consists of.
#
# Note `*` does not cross `/` in gitignore, so `issues/*/*` matches only the
# entries directly inside an issue dir -- prg/ contents stay visible once the
# directory itself is re-included.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
issues/*/*
!issues/*/*.html
!issues/*/*.png
!issues/*/*.svg
!issues/*/*.txt
!issues/*/*.pdf
!issues/*/prg/

# REPRINTS.md is editorial content (which Sonderheft article is a reprint of
# what), not a work log like LOG.md -- it belongs to the issue.
!issues/*/REPRINTS.md

# issue.json is the issue DESCRIPTOR -- kind, binding, page count, where its
# scans live, which colour profile grades them.  Every step of the scan2ocr
# chain reads it (via rules/r000_issue.py) to know which issue it is working
# on, so it is source, not output.
!issues/*/issue.json

# site assets live beside the issues, same depth
!issues/fonts/*
!issues/fav/*
